{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Natural selection is blind and non-teleological; scientific preference is a conscious, critical choice aimed at error elimination.","Organisms die permanently; falsified theories can be revised or resurrected in modified forms.","Biological fitness is relative to a specific environment; scientific corroboration aims for universal explanatory power."],"overturn_conditions":["If one demonstrates that choosing the survivor logically entails a belief in its future success.","If no non-inductive criterion for 'severity' of a test can be defined."],"shared_structure":["Variation generates diverse candidates (theories/organisms).","Severe testing (environmental pressure/experiments) eliminates the unfit/falsified.","Survivors are preferred not because they are proven perfect, but because they have demonstrated resilience.","Future success is not guaranteed, only past failure is ruled out."],"source_memory_refs":["biological_natural_selection"],"transfer_claims":["Preference for the survivor is rational without implying certainty of future survival.","The process explains progress (adaptation/knowledge growth) without requiring induction."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms not as an inductive inference of future reliability, but as a critical selection mechanism analogous to natural selection: we prefer the theory that has survived the severest tests because it contains more empirical content and has withstood attempts to falsify it, making it the best available target for further criticism, without assigning it a higher probability of truth.","counterconditions":["If the preference implies that the theory is more likely to be true (inductive leap).","If the preference relies on a coherent definition of verisimilitude that compares false theories (given Tichý/Miller objections).","If the agent requires a guarantee of practical success (e.g., the bridge will not fall) rather than a commitment to rigorous testing."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Evolutionary Epistemology and Critical Rationalism: The preference is pragmatic and methodological, not logical or probabilistic. Just as natural selection favors organisms adapted to past environments without guaranteeing future survival, critical rationalism favors theories that have survived severe tests without guaranteeing they are true or probable. The 'cost' of accepting this is the abandonment of the desire for positive justification; we act on the best-tested theory knowing it may still be false, simply because it is the most informative and least easily refuted option currently available.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85}],"simulation_proposals":[]}